course: dictionary
lesson: 7473
title: Portable: light enough to carry
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Portable is an adjective when it describes a thing. It means light and small enough to be carried or moved easily, like «a portable speaker» that you can take to the park. Read the words around it to check: a portable heater is one you can move from room to room.
explain: Portable can also be a noun, meaning a thing that is portable, like a portable TV. In Canada and some parts of the US, «portables» are temporary classrooms in the school yard. The word has the same shape in both jobs, so look at the sentence to see which job it is doing.
explain: Some portable things need care. «A portable heater must be kept away from curtains and bedding and never covered.» The warning tells you that a moved object can still be dangerous if you use it badly.
fact: Portable means light and small enough to be carried or moved easily.
fact: «Portable charger», «portable speaker» and «portable toilet» are all things that can be moved from place to place.
fact: As a noun, «a portable» is a portable thing, like a portable computer.
fact: In Canada and some parts of the US, «portables» are temporary classrooms in a school yard.
fact: «A portable heater must never be covered, or it can start a fire.» Keep it away from curtains and bedding.
exercise: «A portable speaker» is small enough to carry. What does portable mean? | Only for use in a classroom | *Light and small enough to be carried easily | Very loud and heavy | Fixed firmly to a wall
exercise: A Canadian student says, «The portables are out in the yard.» What are portables here? | Sports bags for football | *Temporary classrooms in the school yard | Small toys | Heavy machines for building roads
exercise: «A portable heater must be kept away from curtains and bedding.» Why must it be kept away from curtains? | To make it heavier | Because curtains are portable | To help it stay still | *To stop it starting a fire
test: Which of these is a portable thing? | *A portable charger you can put in your bag | A tall tree in the park | A brick wall | A bus stop bench bolted to the ground
test: «A boy swung on a portable football goal; it tipped over on top of him and broke his collarbone.» What does this show? | Collarbones are made of wood | Football goals never move and are always safe | Boys should never play football | *Portable things can move, so they need care to stay safe
exam: Which phrase uses portable to describe a thing? | *a portable speaker you can carry | Walk to the portables after lunch | The portables stand in the yard
